---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { SITE } from "../config";
import { FOODS } from "../data/foods";
import { breadcrumbLd } from "../lib/seo";

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbLd([{ name: "Home", path: "/" }, { name: "About", path: "/about" }]),
  { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE.SITE_NAME, url: SITE.SITE_URL, email: SITE.CONTACT_EMAIL,
    description: `Air fryer cook times and temperatures for ${FOODS.length}+ foods, with a built-in timer.` },
];
---

<Base title={`About ${SITE.SITE_NAME}`} description={`Why ${SITE.SITE_NAME} exists and how we keep our air-fryer times useful and honest.`} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ name: "Home", path: "/" }, { name: "About" }]} />
    <h1 class="text-balance text-3xl font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">About {SITE.SITE_NAME}</h1>
    <div class="prose mt-6">
      <p>
        {SITE.SITE_NAME} exists for one moment: you're standing at the air fryer,
        food in hand, and you just need to know <strong>how long and how hot.</strong>
        Not a memoir. Not ten photos. The number, a timer, and how to tell when it's
        done.
      </p>

      <h2>Why we built it</h2>
      <p>
        Search "air fryer chicken breast" and you'll scroll past a life story, a
        pop-up, and a wall of ads before you find "18 minutes." That pattern is the
        single most-complained-about thing on the recipe internet. So we built the
        opposite: a fast, searchable database where the temperature and time are the
        first thing you see, with a timer you can start on the spot.
      </p>

      <h2>How we handle the numbers — honestly</h2>
      <p>
        Here's the part most charts won't admit: <strong>air fryers vary.</strong>
        Wattage, basket size, and how full it is all change the timing. So we don't
        pretend our times are exact laws. We give tested, sensible starting ranges,
        we tell you to check early, and — crucially — for anything with a food-safety
        temperature (all meat, poultry and fish) we show the
        <a href="/air-fryer-temperature-chart">USDA safe internal temperature</a> to
        cook to. Time is a guide; a thermometer is the truth.
      </p>

      <h2>What you won't find here</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Auto-playing videos or a story before the answer.</li>
        <li>Made-up precision — we use ranges because reality has ranges.</li>
        <li>Advice to eat undercooked meat. We always point you to the safe temperature.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Full details of where our numbers come from are on the
        <a href="/methodology">methodology & safety page</a>. Spot something off?
        <a href="/contact">Tell us</a> — we'll fix it.
      </p>

      <p><a href="/">Find a cook time →</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
